 Russia's war on Ukraine has sparked a boom in global defense-tech investing, particularly around drones. But very few of those startups are actually based and building in Ukraine.

 Buntar Aerospace is an exception. Driving the news: Buntar this week raised $16 million led by U.S.-based Freedom Fund VC, following a $10.4 million round earlier this year.

 The Kyiv-based company makes electric-powered reconnaissance drones that can operate without GPS, plus remote piloting software. Its products are currently in theater. Zoom in: Buntar co-founder Bohdan Sas explains that being "local" helps Buntar shorten the feedback loop, speaking with military units daily, and also provides a deep pool of potential employees who have both civilian and combat experience.

 He also acknowledges that Buntar must take precautions unlike defense-tech startups elsewhere. "In Ukraine, everything can be a target. Russia has even started to target shopping malls and hospitals. But yes, we have a lot of security procedures. For example, in Ukraine we don't have one big, beautiful facility to manufacture stuff. We have many of them and they are dispersed." Behind the scenes: Sas says a lot of the investment proceeds will be used to scale manufacturing, as Ukraine's drone demand has outpaced Buntar's supply.

 The company is likely to soon seek "tens of millions" in additional funding, with a focus on investors who can aid with international expansion. Drone warfare, Sas notes, hit critical mass in Ukraine but will not be limited to this conflict. Fun fact: The company's name is derived from "Star Wars," as "buntar" means "rebel" in Ukrainian. Likewise, its first drone name was a linguistic riff on "Rogue One."

 
 
